[center][size=6][b]Custom Legendary Modifications Remade 2.1[/b][/size]
[size=3]Over 650 legendary effects for weapons and armour - craft them, or hunt for them.[/size][/center]

[size=4][b]What it is[/b][/size]
CLM adds hundreds of legendary modifications you attach at a weapons or armour workbench: elemental hits, spawned allies and enemies, grenade and mine launchers, coloured lasers, movement and stealth effects, and some that are gloriously overpowered. Simple settings decide what you get: everything free, a costly crafting game, balanced legendaries only, or a loot hunt - and a picker to show only the legendaries you want.

[size=4][b]What's new in 2.1[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]The concise view:[/b] too many legendaries felt alike, so the workbench now shows one balanced legendary per group of similar effects - 82 instead of 316 in a new game. Nothing is removed, and gear you own keeps working.
[*][b]About 90 new legendaries:[/b] 23 new armour effects (Night Owl's, Sun-Kissed, Storm Chasin', Glass Cannon, Ironclad, the Radiatin' auras...); effects CLM lacked (Disheartenin', Disarmin', Rallyin', Terrifyin', Midas'...); the 2016 legendaries that went missing before 1.09; and the 2016-17 requests 1.09 called impossible - CQC's three-round bursts, DragonBlade, Necromancer's, Healing Beam, Cap Showerin', Stealthy, Blitzkrieg, Nature's Wrath and more. Thanks to everyone who asked for them.
[*][b]Balance:[/b] balanced now means no stronger than the base game's strongest of its kind (Two Shot, Rapid, Sentinel's). 56 legendaries above that moved to OP; weak ones were brought up to the base game's (Scattering, Burning, Acidic, Escapist's...).
[*][b]Fixes:[/b] BioReactive Explosive explodes on every hit (since 1.09 it only did on kills), Fisto's no longer multiplies across armour pieces, descriptions match what legendaries do, duplicate names and typos fixed.
[*][b]Spawning legendaries are hidden by default[/b] - make "Show spawning legendaries" to see them.
[/list]
The full list is in the changelog.

[size=4][b]What was new in 2.0[/b][/size]
CLM was last updated in 2017. 2.0 is a full overhaul:
[list]
[*][b]Four DLC instead of six:[/b] Automatron, Far Harbor, Contraptions Workshop and Nuka-World. Wasteland Workshop and Vault-Tec Workshop are no longer needed.
[*][b]Loot mode.[/b] The old "No-craft version / WIP" option, finished: legendaries drop as loose mods from anything you kill (vanilla, DLC or modded enemies), starred legendaries drop far more, family holotapes unlock crafting, and OP legendaries need a rare Legendary Core. Drop rate: Low, Standard or Higher.
[*][b]15 new legendaries[/b] built from pieces 1.09 never finished: Lobbin' mines, Hurtin' (1000%), Instant Killin', Medic's, Overchargin', VATS Penetratin', Quick Hands, Marathon Runnin', Focused, Stealth Boy'd and more.
[*][b]Coloured lasers:[/b] Green, Purple, Gold, Orange, Pink and Deep Blue, with matching beams, muzzle flashes, sparks and light.
[*][b]Dozens of fixes:[/b] legendaries that never worked now do, effects match their descriptions, free recipes that should have had a cost, and holotape menu bugs.
[*][b]Settings that work everywhere:[/b] made at any chemistry station, so PlayStation players get the full mod setup for the first time (see below).
[*][b]Legendary Picker:[/b] too many legendaries at the workbench? Pick the families you want at the chemistry station and see only those.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Fallout 4 with four DLC: [b]Automatron, Far Harbor, Contraptions Workshop and Nuka-World[/b].
[*]No script extender (F4SE) needed. Works on the current game and on older versions (1.10.163 and earlier) - no need to unpack the archives.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installing[/b][/size]
Use a mod manager (Vortex or Mod Organizer 2). The installer asks: [b]packed archives[/b] (recommended, any PC game version) or [b]loose files[/b], and the optional [b]F4SE menu[/b].

[size=4][b]Getting started[/b][/size]
Nothing to set up. A new game starts with [b]costly crafting[/b], [b]balanced legendaries[/b] (no OP ones), the [b]main variants[/b], and the [b]concise view[/b] (one legendary per group of similar effects). Attach legendaries in the "Legendary" slot of any weapons or armour workbench.

[size=4][b]Settings - please read[/b][/size]
Settings decide two things: [b]how you get legendaries[/b] and [b]what the workbench shows[/b]. They are [b]items[/b], not menu switches:
[list]
[*]Every setting is a weightless [b]"CLM Setting: ..."[/b] item in your Pip-Boy's Misc tab. [b]Carry it = on. Drop it (or store or sell it) = off.[/b] Carry none = the defaults.
[*]Make them for free at any [b]chemistry station[/b], category [b]"CLM Settings"[/b]. PC and Xbox: the [b](CLM) Configuration Holotape[/b] (given when you leave Vault 111) gives and takes the same items. PC with F4SE: the optional menu (below).
[/list]
[b]How you get legendaries[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Free crafting:[/b] legendaries cost nothing. Without it: they cost components (costly). Replaces Loot mode.
[*][b]Loot mode:[/b] legendaries drop from the enemies you kill instead of being crafted (see Loot mode below). Replaces Free crafting.
[*][b]Low drop rate / Higher drop rate[/b] (loot mode): with neither, Standard.
[*][b]Hidden legendaries drop too[/b] (loot mode): the legendaries the concise view hides can drop as well.
[/list]
[b]What the workbench shows[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Show all legendaries:[/b] every legendary, as before 2.1. Without it: the concise view (below).
[*][b]CLM Group: ...[/b] (category [b]CLM Groups[/b]): shows one whole group of the concise view.
[*][b]Show OP legendaries:[/b] the overpowered ones too. Without it: balanced only.
[*][b]Show variants:[/b] every strength of each legendary (Jumpy 10% to 300%...). Without it: the main ones.
[*][b]Hide perk-style legendaries:[/b] without it, they are shown.
[*][b]Show spawning legendaries:[/b] the legendaries that spawn creatures and people. Without it (and without Show all legendaries) they are hidden, so a new game starts with weapons and armour only. ("Hide spawning legendaries" still hides them even with Show all.)
[*][b]Picker mode:[/b] only the families you pick (see Legendary Picker below). Without it: every family.
[/list]
Good to know:
[list]
[*]A setting you already carry is not offered again. Free crafting and Loot mode replace each other, and so do the two drop rates.
[*]The chemistry station and workbench don't refresh while open: after a change, leave and use them again.
[*]Something missing? Check your Misc tab: a setting may be hiding it, or you already carry the item. Armour legendaries are at the [b]armour[/b] workbench, on pieces with a legendary slot (not clothing such as the Vault suit).
[/list]

[size=4][b]The concise view[/b][/size]
Many legendaries feel alike, so the workbench shows [b]one balanced legendary per group of similar effects[/b] - 33 groups, such as "More damage" (Hurtin' (50%) shows; Hurtin' (25%), Hurtin' (100%), Devastating and Beginner wait in the group) or "Grenades and mines". Where no single legendary stood for its group, a new one does: Opportunist's, Elemental Strikin', Double Tappin', Resistance (All), Vitality, Untouchable (Elements), Radiatin' (Elements). Spawns show each creature's plain Random spawn; the Legendary and Boss spawns are a group of their own.
[list]
[*][b]Nothing is removed.[/b] Gear that has a hidden legendary keeps it.
[*]Make [b]"CLM Setting: Show all legendaries"[/b] to see every legendary again, or a [b]"CLM Group"[/b] item (chemistry station, category CLM Groups) to see one whole group. Each group item lists what it adds.
[*]Variants and OP legendaries are not part of it: Show variants and Show OP legendaries handle those.
[*][b]Saves made before 2.1 keep everything shown:[/b] they get "Show all legendaries" when you load them (PC and Xbox). On PlayStation, make it at the chemistry station.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Loot mode[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Legendaries drop from any enemy you kill (vanilla, DLC or from other mods) as loose mods: attach one at a workbench and it is used up instead of components. Starred legendary enemies drop far more.
[*]CLM holotapes drop too, one per family: carry one to craft its whole family. OP legendaries also need a Legendary Core (weapon or armour), a rare drop.
[*]Standard drop rate, per kill: common 3%, uncommon 1%, rare 0.3%, OP 0.1%.
[*]The legendaries the concise view hides don't drop unless [b]Hidden legendaries drop too[/b] (or [b]Show all legendaries[/b]) is on.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Legendary Picker[/b][/size]
Make [b]"CLM Setting: Picker mode"[/b] and the workbench shows only the families you pick. At the chemistry station, open a [b]CLM Armour[/b], [b]CLM Spawns[/b] or [b]CLM Weapon[/b] category and make a pick (e.g. CLM Weapon: Damage > "CLM Pick: Hurtin' (Weapon)"). Drop a pick to hide that family again; drop Picker mode to see every family. The other settings still apply on top (OP, variants, the concise view); OP-only families are also under [b]CLM OP[/b].

[size=4][b]Optional F4SE menu (PC)[/b][/size]
Have [b]F4SE[/b] and [b]Mod Configuration Menu[/b]? Tick [b]CLM - MCM[/b] in the installer and set everything from the pause menu: the Settings page (crafting mode, drop rate, hidden drops, Show all, OP, variants, Picker mode), the Legendary Picker, a Concise view page with a switch per group, and pages to add or remove any of the perk holotape's 110 perks. Nothing to carry: no setting, pick or group items, no holotapes (turn "Holotapes" on in the menu if you want them). Before removing it, press "Turn settings and picks back into items" in the menu.

[size=4][b]Why the chemistry station?[/b][/size]
In 1.09 the settings were holotape menus run by scripts. PlayStation mods can't use custom scripts, so on PS4 part of the mod couldn't be set up at all. Settings crafted at a chemistry station need no scripts, so CLM works the same on PC, Xbox and PlayStation. The chemistry station is the way for everyone without a script extender - Xbox and PlayStation players, and PC players who don't use F4SE. PC players with F4SE can use the optional menu instead (above).

[size=4][b]PlayStation version[/b][/size]
CLM is also on Bethesda.net for PS4/PS5. PlayStation mods cannot use scripts or new assets, so there:
[list]
[*]Settings are made at the chemistry station (no holotapes); loot mode also needs the CLM Loot Manual, made once there. CLM's perks are "CLM Perk" magazines there too (category CLM Perks): reading one adds the perk.
[*]Saves made before 2.1: make "CLM Setting: Show all legendaries" to keep every legendary shown.
[*]The new laser colours and the Kellogg spawn are left out.
[*]Vampiric and its family heal on every hit except killing blows; Auto-Medical and Aid Patch regenerate health instead of using stimpaks; Face Bustin' does not blow targets apart; BioReactive Explosive and Singularity explode on killing hits only; Midas' leaves ordinary ash.
[*]Effects that are scripts do nothing there: Untouchable, Shavka's Explosive Striking, Ridiculously Absorbin', Sadistic Brawlers, Phoenix, Bloodthirsty, Momentum, Static Charged, CQC, Cinematic Excellence, Cleavin', Decimatin', Necromancer's, Radroachin'. A few others lose one scripted part. Their descriptions say so.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
CLM changes no vanilla records, so it works alongside most mods; loot mode works with enemies from other mods too.
[list]
[*][b]Legendary Effect Overhaul (LEO):[/b] compatible, any load order (checked with LEO 2.1.3). CLM's legendaries use the normal legendary slot (LEO's "L1"); LEO's extra slots are for LEO's own effects. Both loot systems can run together.
[*]Old Crafting Framework patches made for 1.09 aren't needed and no longer apply.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Upgrading[/b][/size]
Your gear keeps its legendaries, DLC ones included, and your holotape settings are turned into setting items on the first load. From 2.0 or 1.09: everything stays shown (you get "Show all legendaries"); some very strong legendaries are now OP, so make "Show OP legendaries" to keep crafting them.

[size=4][b]Open source[/b][/size]
The whole mod - plugin, script sources and the tools used to build it - is on GitHub with its full history, starting from 1.09 exactly as released. Licence: CC BY 4.0 - use it in your own mods, just credit the original author.

